# ADGM Arbitration Regulations 2015 — recognition and enforcement

Source: ADGM Arbitration Regulations 2015. Pinned URL in
`adgm_arbitration_regulations_2015_source.yaml` (ADGM-direct PDF at
assets.adgm.com — NOT the Thomson Reuters–hosted consolidated rulebook
at en.adgm.thomsonreuters.com, which would carry separate compilation
copyright). In force; current.

ATTRIBUTION (per RULES-ATTRIBUTION.md): ADGM Arbitration Regulations
2015, © Abu Dhabi Global Market. Reproduced from the ADGM-published
PDF for academic / research purposes.

Implements New York Convention 1958, Art V, in close parallel to
Singapore IAA s 31.

NUMBERING NOTE: the operative grounds-for-refusal provision in the
current consolidated text is **section 62** (Part 4 — Recognition and
Enforcement). The earlier 2015 enacted version had this material at
section 56; the encoding's enum names were historically `S56_2_*` /
`S56_3_*`. They were renamed to `S62_a_*` / `S62_b_*` to track the
current statutory numbering, which uses `(1)(a)(i)..(vi)` and
`(1)(b)(i)..(ii)`. A second adversarial audit also discovered that the
historical letter-suffixes had been mis-aligned (e.g. `_b_` was
labelled "NaturalJustice" when the source text has (a)(ii) as
"InvalidAgreement" and (a)(iii) as natural justice); the eight-ground
list below now matches the source text letter-for-letter.

## Section 62 — verbatim text

Reproduced verbatim from the ADGM Arbitration Regulations 2015 (Part 4
— The Recognition and Enforcement of Awards):

62. Grounds for refusing recognition or enforcement

(1) Recognition or enforcement of an arbitral award, irrespective of
the State or jurisdiction in which it was made, may be refused by the
Court only if:

  (a) the party making the application furnishes proof that:

      (i)   a party to the arbitration agreement was, under the law
            applicable to it, under some incapacity;

      (ii)  the arbitration agreement is not valid under the law to
            which the parties have subjected it or, failing any
            indication thereon, under the law of the country where the
            award was made;

      (iii) the party against whom the award is invoked was not given
            proper notice of the appointment of an arbitrator or of
            the arbitral proceedings or was otherwise unable to
            present his case;

      (iv)  the award deals with a difference not contemplated by or
            not falling within the terms of the submission to
            arbitration, or it contains decisions on matters beyond
            the scope of the submission to arbitration, provided that,
            if the decisions on matters submitted to arbitration can
            be separated from those not so submitted, that part of the
            award which contains decisions on matters submitted to
            arbitration may be recognised and enforced;

      (v)   the composition of the arbitral tribunal or the arbitral
            procedure was not in accordance with the agreement of the
            parties, or failing such agreement, was not in accordance
            with the law of the country where the arbitration took
            place; or

      (vi)  the award has not yet become binding on the parties, or
            has been set aside or suspended by a competent authority
            of the country in which, or under the law of which, that
            award was made.

  (b) the Court finds that:

      (i)   the subject-matter of the difference is not capable of
            settlement by arbitration under the laws of the Abu Dhabi
            Global Market; or

      (ii)  the recognition or enforcement of the award would be
            contrary to the public policy of the UAE.

(2) If an application for the setting aside or suspension of an award
has been made to a court referred to in subsection (1), the Court may,
if it considers it proper, adjourn its decision and may also, on the
application of the party seeking recognition or enforcement of the
award, order the other party to provide appropriate security.

(3) Any party seeking recourse against an arbitral award made in the
seat of the Abu Dhabi Global Market shall not be permitted to make an
application under subsection (1) if it has made or could have made an
application under section 58 of these Regulations.

The shape of refusal mirrors the Singapore IAA s 31 shape (see
`sg_iaa_s_31.catala_en`); both modules exist because the statutory
references differ and a Stage-2 simulator routes by tribunal-of-filing.
The public-policy gate is jurisdiction-specific (UAE public policy
under ADGM s 62; Singapore public policy under SG IAA s 31(4)(b)).

## Encoding

```catala
declaration enumeration ADGMArbitrationGroundId:
  -- S62_a_i_Incapacity
  -- S62_a_ii_InvalidAgreement
  -- S62_a_iii_NaturalJustice
  -- S62_a_iv_OutsideScope
  -- S62_a_v_TribunalComposition
  -- S62_a_vi_NotBindingOrSetAside
  -- S62_b_i_NotArbitrable
  -- S62_b_ii_PublicPolicy

declaration enumeration ADGMGroundOutcome:
  -- Dismissed
  -- AllowedInPart
  -- AllowedInFull

declaration enumeration ADGMRecognitionDisposition:
  -- RecognitionGranted
  -- RecognitionGrantedInPart
  -- RecognitionRefused

declaration structure ADGMGroundFinding:
  data ground content ADGMArbitrationGroundId
  data court_outcome content ADGMGroundOutcome
  ## s 62(1)(a)(iv) severability proviso. Applies only to OutsideScope
  ## ground (a)(iv). True iff the in-scope decisions can be separated
  ## from the out-of-scope ones.
  data is_severable content boolean

declaration enumeration ADGMApplicationStatus:
  -- ApplicationProperlyMade
  -- ApplicationBarredByS62_3

declaration structure ADGMRefusalDisposition:
  data n_grounds_pleaded content integer
  data n_grounds_dismissed content integer
  data n_grounds_partial content integer
  data n_grounds_full content integer
  data overall_disposition content ADGMRecognitionDisposition
  data award_recognised content boolean
  data application_status content ADGMApplicationStatus

declaration scope ADGMRecognition:
  input grounds content list of ADGMGroundFinding
  ## s 62(3): a party seeking recourse against an ADGM-seated award is
  ## barred from a s 62 application if it has made or could have made
  ## an application under s 58. When this flag is true, the s 62
  ## application is barred regardless of the grounds pleaded.
  input s58_was_or_could_have_been_available content boolean
  output disposition content ADGMRefusalDisposition

scope ADGMRecognition:
  definition disposition equals
    let dismissed equals
      number of (list of g among grounds
                 such that g.court_outcome = Dismissed)
    in
    let partial equals
      number of (list of g among grounds
                 such that g.court_outcome = AllowedInPart)
    in
    let full equals
      number of (list of g among grounds
                 such that g.court_outcome = AllowedInFull)
    in
    ## s 62(1)(a)(iv) severability: a fully-allowed OutsideScope
    ## ground that is severable yields recognition-in-part, not
    ## refusal.
    let full_severable_relief equals
      number of (list of g among grounds
                 such that g.court_outcome = AllowedInFull
                       and g.ground = S62_a_iv_OutsideScope
                       and g.is_severable)
    in
    let full_dispositive equals full - full_severable_relief in
    let app_status equals
      if s58_was_or_could_have_been_available
      then ApplicationBarredByS62_3
      else ApplicationProperlyMade
    in
    ## When the s 62 application is barred by s 62(3), no disposition
    ## on the merits is reached. The encoding emits RecognitionGranted
    ## (the application failed to engage s 62 at all) with
    ## award_recognised=true, plus the application_status flag so
    ## downstream consumers can distinguish "application barred" from
    ## "application succeeded".
    let overall equals
      if s58_was_or_could_have_been_available then RecognitionGranted
      else (if full_dispositive > 0 then RecognitionRefused
            else (if partial > 0 or full_severable_relief > 0
                  then RecognitionGrantedInPart
                  else RecognitionGranted))
    in
    ADGMRefusalDisposition {
      -- n_grounds_pleaded: number of grounds
      -- n_grounds_dismissed: dismissed
      -- n_grounds_partial: partial
      -- n_grounds_full: full
      -- overall_disposition: overall
      -- award_recognised: (full_dispositive = 0)
                            or s58_was_or_could_have_been_available
      -- application_status: app_status
    }
```

## s 62(2) — adjournment and security

Reproduced verbatim from s 62(2): "If an application for the setting
aside or suspension of an award has been made to a court referred to
in subsection (1), the Court may, if it considers it proper, adjourn
its decision and may also, on the application of the party seeking
recognition or enforcement of the award, order the other party to
provide appropriate security."

Parallel to SG IAA s 31(5). Modelled by an analogous scope that
reports whether the adjournment / security power was lawfully
engaged.

```catala
declaration structure S62_2_Inputs:
  data setting_aside_pending_at_seat content boolean
  data adjournment_ordered content boolean
  data security_ordered content boolean

declaration structure S62_2_Output:
  data proceedings_adjourned content boolean
  data security_required content boolean
  data adjournment_engaged_lawfully content boolean

declaration scope ADGM_S62_2_Adjournment:
  input inputs content S62_2_Inputs
  output adjournment content S62_2_Output

scope ADGM_S62_2_Adjournment:
  definition adjournment equals
    let lawful equals inputs.setting_aside_pending_at_seat in
    S62_2_Output {
      -- proceedings_adjourned: inputs.adjournment_ordered and lawful
      -- security_required: inputs.security_ordered and lawful
      -- adjournment_engaged_lawfully: lawful
    }
```

## Used in

ADGM arbitration_recognition / arbitration_enforcement claims (10 coded
judgments in the gold set cite this instrument).

## Tests

```catala
#[test] declaration scope TestADGMRecognitionGranted:
  composite scope ADGMRecognition
  output disposition content ADGMRefusalDisposition

scope TestADGMRecognitionGranted:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  definition composite.s58_was_or_could_have_been_available equals false
  definition composite.grounds equals [
    ADGMGroundFinding { -- ground: S62_b_ii_PublicPolicy  -- court_outcome: Dismissed     -- is_severable: false };
    ADGMGroundFinding { -- ground: S62_a_iv_OutsideScope  -- court_outcome: Dismissed     -- is_severable: false }
  ]
  assertion disposition.overall_disposition = RecognitionGranted
  assertion disposition.award_recognised = true
  assertion disposition.application_status = ApplicationProperlyMade

#[test] declaration scope TestADGMRecognitionRefused:
  composite scope ADGMRecognition
  output disposition content ADGMRefusalDisposition

scope TestADGMRecognitionRefused:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  definition composite.s58_was_or_could_have_been_available equals false
  ## A single fully-allowed public-policy ground is dispositive.
  definition composite.grounds equals [
    ADGMGroundFinding { -- ground: S62_b_ii_PublicPolicy  -- court_outcome: AllowedInFull -- is_severable: false };
    ADGMGroundFinding { -- ground: S62_a_iv_OutsideScope  -- court_outcome: Dismissed     -- is_severable: false }
  ]
  assertion disposition.overall_disposition = RecognitionRefused
  assertion disposition.award_recognised = false

#[test] declaration scope TestADGMSeverabilityRelief:
  composite scope ADGMRecognition
  output disposition content ADGMRefusalDisposition

scope TestADGMSeverabilityRelief:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  definition composite.s58_was_or_could_have_been_available equals false
  ## s 62(1)(a)(iv) severability proviso: a fully-allowed OutsideScope
  ## ground that is severable yields recognition-in-part rather than
  ## refusal — the in-scope decisions can be recognised separately.
  definition composite.grounds equals [
    ADGMGroundFinding { -- ground: S62_a_iv_OutsideScope  -- court_outcome: AllowedInFull -- is_severable: true }
  ]
  assertion disposition.overall_disposition = RecognitionGrantedInPart
  assertion disposition.award_recognised = true

#[test] declaration scope TestADGMS62_3_BarsApplication:
  composite scope ADGMRecognition
  output disposition content ADGMRefusalDisposition

scope TestADGMS62_3_BarsApplication:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  ## s 62(3) bars an applicant who has made or could have made an
  ## application under s 58 from invoking s 62. Even on what would
  ## otherwise be a fully-allowed public-policy ground, the s 62
  ## application is treated as not properly made; recognition is
  ## granted (the application failed to engage s 62) and
  ## application_status flags the bar.
  definition composite.s58_was_or_could_have_been_available equals true
  definition composite.grounds equals [
    ADGMGroundFinding { -- ground: S62_b_ii_PublicPolicy  -- court_outcome: AllowedInFull -- is_severable: false }
  ]
  assertion disposition.overall_disposition = RecognitionGranted
  assertion disposition.award_recognised = true
  assertion disposition.application_status = ApplicationBarredByS62_3
```
